@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the rival who was not in the room
#
# One generalization, formed from three attested claims in three transmissions
# between which no route has been proposed. Its subject is a particular member
# of the list held at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation: the account of
# an empty result that names another operator.
#
# It is filed on its own because the interesting part is not that the account
# exists but that it comes in pairs. A corpus that explains a failure by an
# unseen rival is, in every case here, a corpus that also teaches how to be
# one — so the excuse is not an evasion imported from outside the tradition
# but a consequence of what the tradition itself says people can do.
#

pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The account that names an absent operator, and the same corpus that teaches the part"@en, "Die Erklärung, die einen abwesenden Operierenden nennt, und dasselbe Korpus, das die Rolle lehrt"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission enumerates the accounts available for a session that produced nothing, one of the accounts is the action of a hostile or competing operator who was not present; and the same corpus elsewhere treats that action as an operation somebody deliberately performs, prescribing or describing how it is done. The excuse and the attack are transmitted together, so that the account is available to every practitioner of the transmission and can be neither confirmed nor refuted by anyone who attended the session."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ClaviculaCausaeNonAdventus, pmc:SacrificiumAlienumCorruptum, pmc:BengeFailureCauses ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The witnesses, and each carries the pair in a different way. The Key of Solomon, listing why the spirits may not have come, supposes that they were being conjured in some other place or part of the universe by some other exorcist by the same conjuration — and the book that supposes it is the book that other exorcist would be using, so the attack is the transmission's own procedure in another pair of hands. The Atharvaveda supplies the attack in the clearest form any corpus here offers: let perdition smite his offering before it comes true, let them smite his truth with untruth, let not that meet with success which he yonder offers — and, in the same collection, the hymn in which performers say that desiring to accomplish the sacrifice they did not accomplish it. The Zande list of the eight causes of an oracle contradicting itself contains witchcraft and sorcery, in a transmission whose whole account of misfortune is that witchcraft is something people have and sorcery something people do."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes the pairing more than an observation about human nature. An account of failure that named a natural obstacle — the weather, the season, a bad batch of poison — can be checked by anyone who was there, and the same three transmissions carry such accounts beside this one. This account cannot, and the reason is structural rather than rhetorical: the party named is defined by being elsewhere. A transmission reaches that shape not by evasion but by taking its own claims seriously, since a corpus that teaches an operation performed at a distance on a person who does not know it is being performed has thereby licensed the explanation."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, for transmissions that list the causes of an empty result. What it found on the other side is that the third-party account is not universal: the Chinese burial book assigns its adversities to the year, the season, the ground and the conduct of the descendants and names no rival geomancer; the internal alchemy assigns failure to the practitioner and to the absence of a teacher. The statement is accordingly worded to say what such a list contains where it contains this member, and not that every list does. Whether the presence of the member correlates with the corpus teaching hostile operations at all is the count a later run should make; the three here are consistent with it and three is not a count."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by an operative transmission that names an absent rival among its accounts of an empty result and nowhere in its corpus treats hostile operation as something a practitioner does — the excuse without the attack. It would show the account to be borrowed rather than entailed, which is the opposite of what the statement claims. Weakened, without being refuted, if the three here turn out to be reachable from one another; no route between the Solomonic, the Atharvavedic and the Zande material has been proposed, and the Zande contact question stands open in this holding as undecided."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether such interference occurs. It is a statement about what a corpus makes available to its practitioners, which is why it can be settled by reading and why it belongs here rather than at a protocol."@en .
